        "original_id": "34a006a9-cdd2-54ab-a674-192ecb9823ed",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- Organizations: Vanderbilt University, Emory University, Methodist Episcopal Church\n- People: Cornelius Vanderbilt, John Emory, William Ridley Wills\n- Places: Oxford, Georgia, metropolitan Atlanta, Brownsville, Tennessee\n- Roles: shipping and rail magnate, Methodist bishop\n- Concepts: endowment, relocation, rechartering\n- Buildings: Tennessee Governor's Mansion\n\nFacts:\n- Emory University was founded as Emory College in 1836 in Oxford, Georgia by the Methodist Episcopal Church.\n- Emory College was named in honor of Methodist bishop John Emory.\n- Vanderbilt University was founded in 1873.\n- Vanderbilt University was named in honor of shipping and rail magnate Cornelius Vanderbilt.\n- Cornelius Vanderbilt provided Vanderbilt University with its initial $1 million endowment despite never having been to the South.\n- Emory College relocated to metropolitan Atlanta in 1915 and was rechartered as Emory University.\n- William Ridley Wills was born in Brownsville, Tennessee on March 4, 1897.\n- William Ridley Wills graduated from Vanderbilt University and was a member of the Fugitive literary group.\n- The Tennessee Governor's Mansion is a three-story Georgian-style mansion built in 1929 as a private home for William Ridley Wills and his family.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
      {
        "id": 1,
